{
  "id": "030dtmq",
  "title": "Chapter 12: Periodic Table",
  "order": 12,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "16udf3k",
      "content": "The periodic table is the systematic arrangement of chemical elements based on their atomic numbers, electronic configuration and chemical properties. It is one of the most important tools in chemistry that helps scientists understand the relationships between different elements and predict their behavior."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Dobereiner's Triads",
          "value": "3 Elements Grouped",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Newlands' Octaves",
          "value": "Every 8th Element",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "Mendeleev's Basis",
          "value": "Atomic Mass",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Modern Table Basis",
          "value": "Atomic Number",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "b46usmm"
    },
    {
      "id": "mds7jzq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Need for Classification of Elements"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Dobereiner classified elements with similar chemical properties into sets of three called Triads.",
        "Newlands' Law of Octaves stated that every eighth element has properties similar to the first when arranged in increasing order of atomic mass.",
        "Mendeleev created the first comprehensive periodic table based on atomic masses and predicted properties of undiscovered elements.",
        "The Modern Periodic Law states that properties of elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers.",
        "Periodic trends such as atomic radius, ionization energy, and electronegativity show predictable variations across periods and down groups."
      ],
      "id": "dktxlyb"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vhnnjzy",
      "content": "• As the number of discovered elements increased, it became difficult to study them individually."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xvti6kn",
      "content": "• Scientists needed a systematic way to group elements with similar properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "njyu06b",
      "content": "• The periodic table helps organize elements for easier study and understanding."
    },
    {
      "id": "jvgol13",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Dobereiner’s Triads"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wumxui4",
      "content": "Johann Wolfgang Dobereiner (1829) grouped elements into sets of three called triads based on similar chemical properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "dr7d23t",
      "content": "• Lithium, Sodium, Potassium"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5vc61mo",
      "content": "• Calcium, Strontium, Barium"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "i9ic22i",
      "content": "• Chlorine, Bromine, Iodine"
    },
    {
      "id": "vxvovdo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Newlands’ Law of Octaves"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "19n6lpw",
      "content": "John Newlands arranged elements in order of increasing atomic mass and observed that every eighth element had similar properties."
    },
    {
      "id": "vb5s1kg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Mendeleev’s Periodic Table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wbipe07",
      "content": "Dmitri Mendeleev arranged elements in increasing order of atomic mass and grouped them based on similar chemical properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mtswrt6",
      "content": "• Left gaps for undiscovered elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nrc2gdi",
      "content": "• Predicted properties of unknown elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "w94cmpj",
      "content": "• Grouped elements with similar properties together."
    },
    {
      "id": "9zrywka",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Modern Periodic Table"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xa0tugc",
      "content": "The modern periodic table was developed by Henry Moseley in 1913 based on atomic number."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9n4bs5u",
      "content": "• Elements are arranged according to increasing atomic number."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "9eq9bas",
      "content": "• There are 7 horizontal rows called periods."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "66khdcr",
      "content": "• There are 18 vertical columns called groups."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xw76ioo",
      "content": "• Elements in the same group have similar chemical properties."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Mendeleev's Periodic Table",
        "Modern Periodic Table"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Basis of Classification",
          "Atomic Mass",
          "Atomic Number"
        ],
        [
          "Position of Isotopes",
          "Could not be assigned logical positions",
          "Placed at the same position as they have the same atomic number"
        ],
        [
          "Anomalous Pairs",
          "Existed (e.g., Co placed before Ni)",
          "Resolved based on atomic numbers"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "qtngjiw"
    },
    {
